  "subtitle": "A live-television console whose whole point is one big metal lever the technical director shoves to send one picture away and bring another on air (exact model year unverified)",
  "why_scout_cares": "The Grass Valley 110 vision mixer is the museum's cleanest embodied-performance artifact: a broadcast switcher from the company that, by 1968, had set the industry standard for production switchers. Its defining control is the transition lever — the T-bar — a physical fader the technical director (TD) physically pushes to effect a dissolve, wipe, or cut between two live video buses while the show is going out on air. There is no undo: mid-transition, the TD's hand is literally in the signal path, and the bus being switched to is always the 'hot' one. It fills a wholly unrepresented category — live-broadcast, irreversible performance hardware — and comes with an extraordinary pop-culture hook: a Grass Valley switcher console served as the Death Star superlaser controls in Star Wars (1977), and vintageTEK confirms the model 1600's signature GVG 'T' handle was the lever an actor throws to fire the weapon. Distinct from the Sony BVE-600 edit controller (post-production, offline) already in the collection.",
