    "tags": ["HCI", "Broadcast", "Embodied", "Performance", "Input"],
    "overview": "The Grass Valley Group 110 is a production switcher — what the rest of the world calls a vision mixer — built by the company that defined the category. Grass Valley Group was founded in 1959 by Dr. Donald Hare in Grass Valley, California; it demonstrated its first video product (a distribution amplifier) in 1964 and by 1968 had introduced its first production switcher, which came to define the industry standard. The company merged with Tektronix in 1974, so the switchers of this era were produced under that corporate lineage. The museum's reference image shows a 110 console: a dense field of illuminated source buttons, keys, and the signature transition lever. Note on dating: no primary source was found that pins the 110's exact introduction year. Grass Valley's first production switcher appeared by 1968, and the related 1600 Series was in catalog by 1979; the 110 is placed here as a mid-to-late-1970s model within that span, and the exact year remains unverified.\n\nA production switcher's job is to select between several live video sources and, where needed, composite them together — mixing, wiping, and keying pictures in real time as a broadcast goes out. In the United States the equipment is called a video switcher or production switcher and its operator is the technical director (TD); in most of the rest of the world both the device and its operator are called a vision mixer. The role is roughly the video analogue of an audio mixing console: where an audio engineer fades between microphones, the TD fades and cuts between cameras.\n\nWhat makes the device a genuinely strange interface — and the reason it belongs in this museum — is the transition lever, universally called the T-bar. Unlike a modern software panel, the T-bar is a heavy physical fader. The TD grips it and physically drives the transition from one bus to the other, and because a live show is airing while they do it, the lever is an embodied, irreversible performance control rather than a request to a computer.",
    "deepDive": [
      {
        "heading": "Buses, program, and preset",
        "text": "The core of the machine is the 'bus' — a row of buttons each selecting one video input into a common signal path. The oldest scheme is the A/B mixer, with two equivalent buses and one chosen as the program (on-air) output. Most switchers instead use a flip-flop layout: one bus is always the program bus, the second is the preview (or preset) bus, and the operator can exchange their selected sources. This is the crucial mental model for the T-bar: on a flip-flop switcher the physical position of the transition lever does not indicate which bus is live, because the program bus is always the active — 'hot' — one. The lever is a momentary act, not a persistent state marker, which is exactly why it reads as strange next to modern toggle-style UIs."
      },
      {
        "heading": "The T-bar, an irreversible hand on the signal",
        "text": "The defining control is the transition lever — also called the T-bar or fader bar — an audio-fader-like handle used to transition between two buses. Instead of moving it by hand, the operator can press a button (commonly labeled mix, auto, or auto trans) that performs the transition over a user-defined time; a separate cut or take button swaps preview to program instantaneously. The hand-driven T-bar is the dramatic case: the TD's arm physically sweeps one live picture off the air and brings another on, in full view of a control room, with a fixed duration determined only by how fast they move. There is no undo step and no confirmation dialog. This is the museum's clearest example of performance hardware where the interface is the act, executed under a live broadcast clock."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Cut bar, keying, and the surrounding console",
        "text": "Around the transition lever sits a dense control surface. Sources selected on the preset bus can be switched to program by a cut-bar switch, and a video mixing amplifier allows dissolves between the program and preset buses. Keying lets one signal be inserted over the program picture: the fill is the image seen, the source is the mask that cuts its translucence, and the mask may be derived from chrominance (chroma key), luminance, pattern, or a split. Multiple mix/effects (M/E) sections let an operator compose keys and effects 'offline' and then take them live with one button, and a downstream keyer (DSK) typically keys text and graphics. The whole console is a dense, hardware-only surface of buttons and illuminated tallies — no screen, no menu, every state visible as a lit lamp."
      },
      {
        "heading": "The console and the E-MEM era",
        "text": "By the late 1970s Grass Valley's 1600 Series production switchers were sold as families (1600-1A/1L, 1600-2V, 1600-3K/7K, and so on), with an optional E-MEM (Effects Memory system) that let an operator store digital and analog control-panel selections and auto-transition rates as memory registers for later recall — a landmark move from purely physical memory to machine memory for live switching. The museum's cover image is the keyer control panel of a GVG 200 (a related panel), and the 1600 is the model tied to the museum's most famous claim to fame. The transition lever remained the human, physical heart of all of them."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Commercial fate and impact",
        "text": "Grass Valley's switchers became the reference standard for live television production and the company dominated the broadcast plant for decades, passing through Tektronix (1974 –1999), a reincorporation as Grass Valley Group, Inc., Thomson (2002), Francisco Partners (2010–2011), Belden (2014, merged with Miranda), and finally Black Dragon Capital (2020). The transition lever and bus architecture the 110 and its contemporaries taught are still the mental model of every modern switcher panel — hardware and software alike — which is why the T-bar survives as the industry's most recognizable physical control."
      }
    ],
